 ## Overview
-Brick-based storage and caching decomposes volumetric datasets larger than available GPU texture memory into smaller, fixed-size sub-volumes called bricks, enabling out-of-core visualization by treating GPU memory as a cache for these bricks [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. It solves the hardware limitation where a single 3D texture cannot hold the entire volume, minimizing expensive data transfers from slower host memory [S4, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by providing the foundational memory management and subdivision logic upon which specific traversal (e.g., [Brick-based Volume Raycasting](3341-brick-based-volume-raycasting.md)), caching policies (e.g., [Brick Caching with LRU Replacement](879-brick-caching-with-lru-replacement.md)), and multi-resolution strategies (e.g., [Level-of-Detail Volume Rendering via 3D Textures](1706-level-of-detail-volume-rendering-via-3d-textures.md)) are built.
+Brick-based storage and caching decomposes volumetric datasets larger than available GPU texture memory into smaller, fixed-size sub-volumes called bricks, enabling out-of-core visualization by treating GPU memory as a cache for these bricks [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. It solves the hardware limitation where a single 3D texture cannot hold the entire volume, ensuring that expensive data transfers from slower host memory are minimized [S4, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by explicitly managing the cache lifecycle (e.g., LRU replacement) and handling boundary interpolation via overlap, whereas [Tile-based Volume Ray Marching](1678-tile-based-volume-ray-marching.md) focuses on tile-level traversal and [Spatial Indexing and Ordering](1797-spatial-indexing-and-ordering.md) focuses on acceleration structures for traversal rather than memory residency management.
 
