 ### [Level-of-Detail Volume Rendering via 3D Textures](1706-level-of-detail-volume-rendering-via-3d-textures.md)
-This method provides an adaptive level-of-detail (LOD) selection algorithm for multi-resolution volume rendering using 3D texture mapping. It renders volume data in a region-of-interest at high resolution while progressively lowering the resolution for areas further away, enabling interactive exploration of large-scale datasets. Unlike approaches that rely on user-specified error tolerances or spherical shells, this technique guarantees consistent interpolation between different resolution levels and explicitly fixes rendering artifacts (such as light/dark bands) at brick boundaries using polygon clipping and opacity correction.
+Level-of-Detail (LOD) Volume Rendering via 3D Textures is a method for interactive visualization of large-scale volumetric datasets by adaptively selecting resolution levels for individual bricks based on visual importance criteria. It solves the problem of texture memory exhaustion and excessive sampling rates in large volumes by constructing a local multi-resolution hierarchy for each brick, allowing regions of interest to be rendered at high resolution while distant or less opaque regions are rendered at coarser levels. Unlike spherical-shell approaches, this method uses planar slicing parallel to the image plane and guarantees continuous interpolation and correct opacity accumulation at brick boundaries through explicit texture adaptation and polygon clipping.
 
