 ## Background
-Bricking was introduced as a solution to the limited texture memory of early GPUs (e.g., NVIDIA GeForce 6 with $512^3$ resolution limits and 256MB memory caps) [S1, unofficial]. It enabled the visualization of volumes far exceeding hardware constraints by treating GPU memory as a cache for on-demand loaded bricks [S2, unofficial][S6, unofficial]. The technique has since evolved to include multi-resolution hierarchies [S1, unofficial], specialized caching policies like LRU [S2, unofficial], and integration with compression schemes for segmentation volumes [S1, unofficial]. It is widely used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and real-time rendering engines where large datasets must be explored interactively.
+Bricking was introduced to overcome the limitations of 3D texture memory sizes in early GPU hardware (e.g., NVIDIA GeForce 6 with a $512^3$ resolution limit and 256MB memory limit) [S1, unofficial]. The technique allows visualization of volumes that exceed these limits by treating the GPU memory as a cache for bricks loaded from disk [S2, unofficial][S6, unofficial]. The approach has been widely adopted in various forms, including CPU-side LRU caching [S2, unofficial], GPU-side N³-trees [S1, unofficial], and web-based multi-level caching with prefetching [S1, unofficial]. It is used in real-time volume rendering, medical imaging visualization, and scientific data analysis.
 
